A flow of rich text, rendered by html.

The span family is an ordinary component tree and this is a platform
implementing it: `lib/platform/html/html.sngl` names the element html
already has for what each member means, so `bold` is `<strong>` and a link is
`<a>`, and the nesting an author wrote carries straight over. The cascade is
CSS's own -- the five typography fields a `SpanStyle` carries are exactly the
ones a browser inherits -- so nothing flattens the tree and no pass knows what
a span is.

Four claims are in the output below and each is here because it could go
wrong somewhere else:

  - **Escaping.** `<`, `&` and a quote in an author's words come out as
    entities, which is the reason a flow is rendered by a platform rather than
    by a library writing markup into `innerHTML`.
  - **Literal whitespace.** Two spaces are two spaces and a newline ends the
    line. html is the one host that would otherwise collapse them, so the flow
    carries `white-space: pre-wrap` and the static renderer stops indenting
    inside it -- the pretty-printer's own newlines would be visible text.
  - **Both nesting orders are one answer.** `monospace { link { … } }` and
    `link { monospace { … } }` are `<code><a>` and `<a><code>`: different
    trees, same words in the same face leading to the same place.
  - **A role is a semantic.** A heading announces as one, a quotation is
    quoted, a code sample is preformatted. That is what `role` is *for*, as
    against `style`, which is what the block components set beside it.

`image` is the one member whose description is a string rather than spans: no
formatting is available in one, every host that has a description has a plain
attribute, and the quote and ampersand in this fixture's `alt` are there to
show the attribute is escaped like any other.

`token` emits a class and never a color: what a keyword looks like is the
page's to say, and a document naming one would be unreadable against a theme
it never heard of.

bubbletea is the third target and the first host with no inheritance of its
own: lipgloss renders a string and hands back a string, so the cascade an
author wrote is flattened in the emitter and each run of words is rendered
exactly once, with the style it ends up with. Rendering a joined flow a second
time is not the tidier alternative it looks like -- the runs inside it have
left their reset sequences in the string, and an outer color would stop at the
first of them -- which is why a flow's own typography seeds the words rather
than styling the block, and why the block style below carries padding and a
background and no weight.

Three things a terminal answers differently and each is visible above. It has
one face and one size, so `monospace` renders as the words it was already
going to render and a heading is bold rather than large. It cannot follow a
link, so the URL is dropped and the words are underlined, which is the trade
`ui.link` already makes. And a `token` is one of the terminal's own sixteen
colors rather than a value this document chose -- the same argument the html
class makes, answered with the palette the reader's terminal already has.

fyne is the fourth and the one that needed a widget written for it. Its rich
text is a flat list of segments, so the cascade is flattened -- at render time
rather than at build time, because the tree is what a reactive program mutates
and a `SetText` against a nested span is all that reaches the emitted code when
state moves. Three things Fyne does not do for itself are in
`pkg/go/fynetext`: that flattening; a run's color and size, which a segment
names through a *theme* and never carries as a value; and underline and
strikethrough, which Fyne draws nowhere at all.

What is in the generated code below is the tree, and it is the ordinary fyne
path -- a constructor, a Model field, `Add`, a setter per reactive prop --
because a span is a value with an `Add` and a `SetText`, which is all this
platform has ever asked a node to be. The one thing its Spec cannot be is
*data*: a run is constructed with the style it was written with, and which
fields a `SpanStyle` literal set is a question about the literal, so
`markup.go` builds the Spec from the node's own props. The style reaches the
constructor as a builder chain and not a record, because only the first name
needs qualifying and a chain spends that once.

The block styling goes the way every fyne node's does, through a generated
theme: a heading is 32px and bold, a code sample has its background, a caption
its color, and a quotation is italic -- that last one only since this change,
`fyneStyle` having read `fontWeight` and not `fontStyle`, so a quote came out
upright on this target and slanted on every other.

gtk4 is the fifth, and the one target where the *host* resolves the cascade:
Pango markup nests, so `<b><i>x</i></b>` is what `bold { italic { … } }` means
and the tree an author wrote is the tree GTK is handed. Nothing is flattened,
and what this platform builds is the string.

A flow is therefore one `GtkLabel` and a span is no widget at all -- neither
primitive names a GIR class, and the span nodes reach the walk and emit
nothing. The markup is a Go *expression*: a literal run is escaped where it is
written and a run whose words come from state is `gtk4rt.Escape(expr)`
concatenated with the rest, which is why reactivity costs a single
`LabelSetMarkup` and no span in the Model at all
(`testdata/markup_reactive.txtar` is that half).

The flow's own typography is the outer `<span>`, which is also the seed Pango
inherits from -- and it is the only part of a `ui.Style` this target has ever
answered, gtk4 having no per-widget styling and `OnPropAssign` dropping the
prop outright. So a heading is large and bold here and a code sample still has
no background.

A `token` is a Pango foreground, which is what the family's own declaration
nominates for this host: Pango markup has no classes and no theme names, so a
color is the only thing it can be told.

android is the sixth and the last, and it is the best-matched host of them
all: a Compose `SpanStyle` carries color, size, family, weight and slant,
leaves every one of them unset by default, and merges overlapping styles with
an unset field leaving the enclosing one standing -- which is exactly what
`markup.SpanStyle`'s `inherit` members mean. So nothing is flattened, the
nesting an author wrote is the nesting `withStyle` writes, and the *host*
resolves the cascade for the second time in this file.

A span is a piece of an `AnnotatedString` *value* rather than a composable, so
this target looks like gtk4 and not like the other three: a flow is one `Text`
and neither primitive is a call. The flow's two Styles are the ones `ui.text`
already passes -- the Modifier for its box, the `TextStyle` for the typography
its words start from, which is the seed Compose inherits from rather than one
this emitter applies.

`textDecoration` is the one field the host does not merge, and the one thing
carried down the walk: Compose applies a decoration as a single span setting
both of the paint's flags, so the `strike` written inside the `underline`
above writes the whole accumulated set rather than its own -- which is what
the `combine` below is, and what stops the inner run turning the outer rule
off.

A `link` is a real link here, the second target after html where it is:
`withLink(LinkAnnotation.Url(...))` makes the range tappable and `Text`
follows it. What Compose does not do is style one, so the underline is said in
the family's own vocabulary, in the override.

A `token` is a role in the Material color scheme, which is the answer fyne
gives in its own vocabulary -- an application that themes its app themes its
code samples with it, and light and dark come out right without this compiler
naming a value. What a scheme does not have is seven hues, so the kinds are
grouped by what they are: a keyword is the primary accent, the names of things
share the secondary, the literals share the tertiary, and a comment is
onSurfaceVariant. Four targets now hand the palette to the reader and one,
gtk4, still cannot.

`image` renders its description in brackets, as bubbletea, fyne and gtk4 all
do. Compose has `InlineTextContent` and could draw one -- it is the only host
here that could -- but it needs a painter per source, which is the same thing
`ui.image` has no answer for on this platform.

Both html modes are here and both write the same markup. Route mode renders
the page server-side through a second emitter, so it is the one that could
disagree -- and it did: a run's style is a set of CSS declarations rather than
an attribute value, and a prop neither literal nor state-dependent is refused
outright there, so the build failed until both renderers asked one function for
an element's inline CSS.

deny * `innerHTML` -- text reaches the page as text, never as markup
deny * `<richText` -- the family is implemented, not emitted under its own name
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `Bold\(true\)\.Render\(content_` -- a flow's weight seeds its words; applied to the joined string, the first inner reset would end it partway along the line
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `JoinHorizontal` -- a flow concatenates its words, where joining them would put each run in a column of its own
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `fynetext\.SpanStyle` -- a run's style is a builder chain, so the alias this file gave the runtime package is spent on one name
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `\.No(Bold|Italic|Mono)\(\)` -- a field the run left alone contributes nothing; `inherit` arriving as `normal` would un-bold the words around it
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `Escape\(` -- every run here is a literal, and a literal is escaped where it is written
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `LabelSetLabel` -- a flow's words are markup, and set_label would show the tags rather than obey them
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `(?m)^\s*(Flow|Span)\(` -- a flow is one Text and a span is a piece of its value; neither primitive is a composable
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `MaterialTheme.colorScheme.error` -- a scheme's error color is not a syntax color, whatever a token would otherwise have to share
-- doc.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import md "sngl:ui/markup"

output {
    go {
        html
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="doc") {
    ui.vbox {
        md.heading1 {
            md.text(value="Title")
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.text(value="a < b & \"c\"  two spaces\nand a newline")
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.monospace {
                md.link(href="https://example.com/?a=1&b=2") {
                    md.text(value="mono link")
                }
            }
            md.link(href="https://example.com/") {
                md.monospace {
                    md.text(value="link mono")
                }
            }
        }
        md.codeBlock {
            md.token(kind=md.Token.keyword) {
                md.text(value="func")
            }
            md.text(value=" ")
            md.token(kind=md.Token.function) {
                md.text(value="main")
            }
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.underline {
                md.strike {
                    md.italic {
                        md.text(value="all three")
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.text(value="before ")
            md.image(alt="an \"icon\" & more") {
                ui.remote(url="/icon.png")
            }
            md.text(value=" after")
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.run(style={color=#336699, fontSize=20px, fontWeight=md.Weight.bold}) {
                md.text(value="a styled run")
            }
        }
        md.quote {
            md.text(value="quoted")
        }
        md.caption {
            md.text(value="a caption")
        }
    }
}
